A||||4||mshill||strange miss at 2300 rpm...||||1||
Z||000000||mshill||04-18-2000||01:28 AM||||My 86SE V6 4 speed has a strange miss at 2300 rpm in fourth gear.  Here's the history... rebuilt 2.8 has 250 miles on it, +1 computer friendly cam, ported exhaust manifolds, no catalytic convertor, stock muffler.  I checked the  vacuum lines and EGR valve functionality with a vacuum pump.<P>I just swapped my stock 14 inch rims and tires for some 15 inch rims with 50 series tires.<P>While driving on the freeway for the first time sim=nce the tire swap, the car had a bad miss at 2300 rpm while cruising.  The speedo read 60 mph, but with the 50 series tires my wheel diamter is about .5 to .75 inches smaller so I wasn't going 60 but approximately 57 (5% difference in circumference).  The car started to act like it was running out of gas, bucking and missing a little.  If I give it more pedal it accelerated fine and if I cruised at a higher or lower speed it was fine, just at that one speed/rpm combination.<P>The fuel filter has less than 1000 miles on it and the pump was replaced at the same time.<P>Does the ECM take into account the speed when tuning the engine?  Could the change in the diameter of the tires affect ECM performance?<BR>Any other ideas? Maybe a flat spot in the TPS? <P>Any help would be appreciated?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-18-2000||09:11 AM||||I'd check the TPS. Yes, it could have a bad spot in it.(A jittery TPS can be hard to verify. may be esier to just replace it.)<P>Yes the ECM does know about vehicle speed. Exactly how it's used and how critical accuracy is varries from ECM to ECM and even PROM version to PROM version.<P>If the TPS looks ok, try putting the old tires back on for a few days. If it's the ECM that should tell you for sure. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||04-18-2000||11:55 AM||||Ogre,  I swapped the TPS out with my 86GT and it still did the same thing once it got good and warm.  I am convinced that it is something related to the ECM or and ECM controlled component.  It only did it once the engine got warmed up good (closed loop mode) so it isn't like a fuel filter clog.<BR>I also have a feeling that it is probably not the tire size, because I have some form of the problem in all gears, and remember that with my stock tires I would experience the same affect (bucking) at speeds just above idle in first and second gears (like in parking lots).<P>It is at the very top of the throttle (just barely depressed to keep the cruise speed on a very mild downhill slope).  My idle has a definite lope between 850 and 1000 rpm and it isn't just a mild variance, but cycles about every second, as if the IAC was not reacting smoothly.<P>I think I am going to swap out the IAC valve to see what happens.  I am not too concerned since you just need to let off or depress the throttle to get rid of the problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-18-2000||12:18 PM||||just for giggles check the distributor shaft. make sure it's not sloppy. I've seen some pretty odd things happen if the shaft get worn(or isn't rebuilt right)<P>I'd say hook up an ECM scanner. With this kind of problem you should be abe to see something going on. A goofy coolant or air charge temperature sensor could also be an issue, as could bad wiring on either sensor circuit. (Bad wire will skew the sensor resistance even tho the sensor is OK.)<P>The IAC is built in such a way that unless it's totally filthy or damaged it is likely going to work. Stepper motors don't tend to lag. They have much greater torque than normal electric motors of the same size. I supose it's posible you got a bad one in durring the rebuild.<P>It's interesting that it only does this when warm. Makes me wonder if you've got a vacuum leak swelling open as things warm. Rubber parts in the PCV valve line are great for this. an under torqued intake bolt could do it too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||04-18-2000||06:16 PM||||Also...<P>- check base timing<BR>- check for vacuum leaks<BR>- check EGR operation <P><BR>||||reg||3
